---
/*
 * Invite — the closing card: what it costs, and how you actually get in today.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * TWO THINGS WERE DELETED HERE, AND THE DELETIONS ARE THE POINT OF THIS PASS.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * 1. THE SIX-ROW FACTS TABLE. It ran Free tier / Sign-in / Cost / Shape / Repository /
 *    Licence as a key-value list down the left half of the card. Every row was true and
 *    checked, and together they were a spec sheet — the exact artefact the consumer brief
 *    rules out, sitting in the last screenful where the page should be asking for one thing.
 *    Two rows survive as prose because they carry weight a sentence can hold: the free tier
 *    (it is the offer) and the licence plus repo link (it is checkable, and it is the only
 *    outbound proof on the page). Four rows went:
 *      Sign-in "GitHub" — the strongest cut. The old note beside it conceded it was
 *        NOT VERIFIED: no auth provider is enabled in supabase/config.toml, so the row named
 *        an intended flow as if it were a wired integration. A fact that needs a disclaimer
 *        in a comment does not belong in a table on a landing page. /app is where sign-in
 *        is actually offered for the primary create path (and /start remains only as a
 *        backward-compatible on-ramp), and that is where the claim can be made against real
 *        screens.
 *      Cost "No card, no billing" — folded into the lede, where it reads as reassurance
 *        rather than as a specification.
 *      Shape "A CLI and a hosted service" — the reader has just been shown five commands and
 *        a feed panel. Restating the shape in a table row after demonstrating it is the
 *        page explaining what it has already shown.
 *      Repository as a row — kept as a link, lost the row.
 *
 * 2. THE EMAIL FORM. It validated, it announced honestly, and it never rendered a success
 *    state — it was a careful, well-built control that did nothing, and it said so three
 *    times in one card. That care was the tell. A button that cannot act is a dead affordance
 *    however honestly it is labelled, and a consumer page closes by pointing at the real
 *    door, not by asking for an address it has nowhere to put.
 *    SUPERSEDED (dead, 2026-07-30 app-first): "The site already has two real doors —
 *    /download and /start — and the header CTA points at the second of them."
 *    CURRENT: the primary create door is /app; /download remains the technical setup door;
 *    /start is backward-compatible only, not a primary CTA. The close points at /app and
 *    /download and the form is gone, along with its script and its outcome region.
 *
 *    IF A WAITLIST IS EVER WANTED HERE, it needs a server first. Re-adding the front end
 *    without one puts back the thing that was removed.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHERE THE SURVIVING FACTS WERE CHECKED, all in supabase/functions/command/index.ts:
 *   3 live workspaces .... FREE_TIER_WORKSPACE_LIMIT = 3 (:330), counted against rows with
 *                          archived_at IS NULL (:2325) — archiving one frees the slot, which
 *                          is why the copy says "live" and not just "three".
 *   verified identity .... a non-human credential is refused, then an unverified one
 *                          (:2183-2186); identityVerified is email_confirmed_at.
 *   no card .............. no billing surface exists in this repo. RE-MEASURED 2026-07-27,
 *                          not inherited: stripe|billing|payment|subscription|invoice|
 *                          \bprice\b over src/, supabase/functions/, supabase/migrations/ and
 *                          package.json returns 0 lines. Positive control on the SAME paths
 *                          in the same invocation: "workspace" returns 684, so the grep runs
 *                          and the zero is a measurement rather than a wrong path. (An
 *                          earlier note here recorded the control as 1002; that was a
 *                          different counting method, so the number is restated as measured
 *                          rather than carried over.)
 *   MIT .................. package.json:36 — "license": "MIT".
 *   the repo is public ... github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm answered 200 to a logged-out
 *                          request, with a made-up repo path returning 404 as the control.
 *
 * SIGN-UP IS OPEN. SWARM_SELF_SERVE=1 has been set in production since 2026-07-28, and the
 * current fleet's workspace was created through that path. This card therefore makes signup
 * the primary action and states only the measured terms: email or GitHub, three live
 * workspaces, no card.
 *
 * The retired heading, kept dead so nobody re-derives it: ~~"There is no signup yet."~~ It
 * was written when the endpoint genuinely did not exist. `create_workspace` is handled by
 * createSelfServeWorkspace() now, dispatched ahead of route resolution because a brand-new
 * caller has no membership to resolve against. The retired line stays retired.
 */

import Button from "../ui/Button.astro";
---

<section class="section section--tight invite" id="invite" aria-labelledby="invite-title">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="card card--lg card--raised invite__card js-reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">What it costs</p>

      <h2 class="h-section invite__title" id="invite-title">Create your workspace, free.</h2>

      <p class="lede invite__lede">
        Sign up now with email or GitHub. You can have up to three live workspaces, and
        CommonSwarm never asks for a card.
      </p>

      <p class="invite__status">
        Start with one workspace, connect the AI assistant you already use, and let it tell
        the rest of your team what it is working on.
      </p>

      <div class="row invite__ctas">
        <Button href="/app" variant="primary" size="lg">Create your workspace</Button>
        <Button href="/download" variant="secondary" size="lg">Technical setup</Button>
      </div>

      <p class="invite__meta">
        Open source, MIT.
        <a
          class="invite__a"
          href="https://github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Ridge-io/cloud-swarm<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .invite {
    padding-block: clamp(var(--s-8), 3.5vw, var(--s-12));
  }

  /* One column, and centred. The card was a two-column grid because it held a facts list and
     a form; with both gone, a 1.15fr / 1fr split would be a layout with nothing to lay out.
     Everything here is one short column of copy ending in the page's last two buttons, so it
     is centred and capped at a measure rather than stretched across the card. */
  .invite__card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .invite .invite__title {
    font-size: var(--t-2xl);
    margin-block: var(--s-3) var(--stack-sm);
    max-width: 18ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .invite__lede {
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    line-height: var(--lh-lg);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* Set below the lede rather than beside it: it is the caveat on the offer, and a caveat
     that shares a line with the offer reads as part of the pitch. */
  .invite__status {
    margin-block-start: var(--stack-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .invite__ctas {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6);
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* On one column the two buttons hug their own labels and come out different widths, which
     reads as unfinished rather than as hierarchy. Below 30rem they share the full column —
     the same rule, for the same reason, as the hero's CTA row. */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .invite__ctas {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      inline-size: 100%;
    }
  }

  .invite__meta {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  /* The repo is the one fact here that is also a place you can go. Monospace because it is a
     repo path, not prose. */
  .invite__a {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--border-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    transition:
      color var(--dur-2) var(--ease-standard),
      text-decoration-color var(--dur-2) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .invite__a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-bright);
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }
</style>
